# The integration image does `COPY . .`, so anything not excluded here is baked
# into a layer. Two reasons that matters: image size, and the fact that a layer
# is readable by anyone who can pull the image.

# Version control and CI metadata.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.dockerignore

# Credentials. The image build takes its Gitea module token as a BuildKit
# secret, which is mounted on tmpfs for a single RUN and never lands in a layer
# (see test/integration/Dockerfile). These patterns are the second line of
# defence: if a credentials file is ever written into the working tree, it must
# not be copyable into an image.
#
# Every pattern is **-prefixed on purpose. .dockerignore matches whole path
# segments and a bare `*` does not cross `/`, so `.env` and `*.pem` would
# exclude ONLY the context root — leaving .devcontainer/.env and
# test/fixtures/server.key to be swept in by `COPY . .`.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/gomodauth*
**/secrets/

# Build output and local scratch — rebuilt inside the image anyway.
#
# These mirror .gitignore's build-artifact entries, and the mirroring is the
# point. A developer's working tree normally holds a compiled ./frostdkg; git
# ignores it, but `COPY . .` did not, so `make integration-test` shipped
# whatever binary happened to be lying around into the image. One built in
# March against Go 1.26.1 put 31 CRITICAL/HIGH CVEs into the trivy gate —
# findings that belonged to a stale local artifact, not to anything the repo
# actually builds or ships. CI never saw it (fresh checkout), which is exactly
# what makes it the kind of failure that wastes an afternoon.
/frostdkg
bin/
dist/
build/
vendor/
coverage/
coverage*.html
*.out
*.test
*.log
*.exe
*.so
*.dylib
*.wip
go.work
go.work.sum

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
